        "idea": "Binary search on target KVPR with feasibility check via bin packing",
        "description": "The objective is minimize max over GPUs of W_g/(M - S_g) where W_g = sum r_j/s_j, S_g = sum sizes. Fix a threshold T; a GPU is feasible under T if placing a set of models keeps W_g <= T*(M - S_g). Binary search on T and check feasibility by packing models into at most gpu_num GPUs using a greedy/first-fit on the linearized constraint (each model j consumes size s_j and weight w_j; GPU capacity pair (mem, T*(M-S_g))). Use first-fit-decreasing on w_j/s_j ratio with the feasibility test 'W_g + w_j <= T*(M - (S_g + s_j))'. Binary search ~40 iterations gives near-optimal max KVPR.",
        "what_to_optimize": "primary evaluator score (minimize max KVPR across GPUs)",
        "cautions": "Handle models that alone exceed feasibility (must still place them; use max over single-model GPUs as lower bound). Ensure exact-fit memory edge cases (denominator zero) treated carefully; keep T range [min single-model KVPR, sum all weights / small epsilon].",
        "approach_type": "custom binary search + first-fit bin packing"
      },
      {
        "idea": "Local search: greedy initial placement followed by swap/move hill climbing on max KVPR",
        "description": "Start from the current greedy placement. Then iteratively improve: repeatedly find the GPU with maximum KVPR, and try moving one of its models to another GPU or swapping a model with a model from another GPU; accept any move that reduces the max KVPR (or lexicographically reduces the sorted KVPR vector). Continue until no improving move exists or an iteration cap (e.g., 500) is reached. Tie-break by second-highest KVPR to enable cascading improvements.",
        "what_to_optimize": "primary evaluator score; also reduces second-highest KVPR for robustness",
        "cautions": "Always check memory feasibility before a move; keep O(n^2) move evaluation cheap by caching per-GPU W and S sums. Deterministic tie-breaking to avoid infinite loops.",
